Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Cardiologist (Lead) – Specializes in the evaluation and management of acute coronary syndromes (ACS). This member is crucial in diagnosing the condition and initiating immediate treatment strategies, including medication and potential interventions such as angioplasty.): 
 Member 2 (Emergency Medicine Physician – Provides acute care assessment, stabilizes the patient in the emergency department, and manages immediate life): threatening situations, ensuring proper monitoring and interventions are in place.
 Member 3 (Pharmacist – Focuses on medication reconciliation and ensuring the safe and effective use of medications upon admission, especially in relation to the specific drug interactions with the patient’s current medications.): 

Group 2 -  Ongoing Management Team (OMT)
 Member 1 (Interventional Cardiologist (Lead) – Oversees percutaneous coronary interventions and is responsible for monitoring and managing post): procedure care, ensuring that the patient stabilizes and responds well to treatment.
 Member 2 (Hospitalist – Coordinates the inpatient management including medication adjustments and follow): up plans, ensuring seamless transitions from acute care to discharge.
 Member 3 (Cardiac Nurse Educator – Provides patient and family education focusing on heart health, medications post): discharge, lifestyle modifications, and symptom management to prevent readmission.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Cardiologist (Lead) – Offers expert commentary on the overall treatment plan based on the patient's unique circumstances and the latest guidelines in cardiovascular medicine, guiding the final decisions on medications.): 
 Member 2 (Clinical Pharmacologist – Assesses the appropriateness of the medication regimen post): angioplasty and recommends the addition of antiplatelet agents (e.g., Clopidogrel) to prevent further cardiac events.
 Member 3 (Discharge Planner/Social Worker – Addresses any social determinants of health impacting the patient’s adherence to the medication and follow): up care, ensuring that the patient has the resources needed for successful recovery after discharge.


